**Location**: [packages/a2a-server/src/agent/task.ts#L132](https://github.com/google-gemini/gemini-cli/blob/main/packages/a2a-server/src/agent/task.ts#L132)

### Why it's important
`Config` is a shared configuration instance passed across different contexts in the CLI or multiple parallel execution tasks. Mutating this shared config inside the constructor of a single `Task` introduces side effects that permanently override the fallback model handler behavior globally across other concurrent running sessions or tasks, causing them to unexpectedly fail or drop back.

### Proposed Fix
Instead of directly modifying the passed configuration object, clone or shadow the `config` parameters, or implement task-level overrides inside the routing/fallback logic without mutating shared state.

### Description
In `ShellExecutionService`, when wait processing completes normally, a `Promise.race` is used to settle the execution loop between the output processing chain and an abort trigger:

```typescript
const processingComplete = processingChain.then(() => 'processed');
const abortFired = new Promise<'aborted'>((res) => {
  if (abortSignal.aborted) {
    res('aborted');
    return;
  }
  abortSignal.addEventListener('abort', () => res('aborted'), {
    once: true,
  });
});

Promise.race([processingComplete, abortFired]).then(() => {
  finalize();
});
```

**Location**: [packages/core/src/services/shellExecutionService.ts#L1311-L1313](https://github.com/google-gemini/gemini-cli/blob/main/packages/core/src/services/shellExecutionService.ts#L1311-L1313)

### Why it's important
If the command finishes successfully (`processingComplete` resolves), the callback `() => res('aborted')` is never removed from the `abortSignal`. Since `once: true` only unregisters the listener when the `abort` event actually fires, this leaves a dangling reference on the session-long `abortSignal`, leading to a slow memory leak over multiple commands.

### Proposed Fix
Unregister the event listener in a `.then()` cleanup handler:
```typescript
const onAbort = () => res('aborted');
abortSignal.addEventListener('abort', onAbort, { once: true });
...
Promise.race([processingComplete, abortFired]).then(() => {
  abortSignal.removeEventListener('abort', onAbort);
  finalize();
});
```

### Description
When an MCP server requires OAuth authentication, `oauth-provider` starts a local callback loopback server using `startCallbackServer` to receive authorization codes. It then prompts the user for consent. If the user declines consent, the provider throws a `FatalCancellationError`, but the callback server is never shut down:

```typescript
const callbackServer = startCallbackServer(pkceParams.state, preferredPort);
...
const userConsent = await getConsentForOauth(...);
if (!userConsent) {
  throw new FatalCancellationError('Authentication cancelled by user.'); // callbackServer is leaked
}
```

**Location**: [packages/core/src/mcp/oauth-provider.ts#L376](https://github.com/google-gemini/gemini-cli/blob/main/packages/core/src/mcp/oauth-provider.ts#L376) and [L452](https://github.com/google-gemini/gemini-cli/blob/main/packages/core/src/mcp/oauth-provider.ts#L452)

### Why it's important
Declining consent leaves a live HTTP server listening on a local port in the background for up to 5 minutes (until its internal timeout expires). This leaks resources and leaves a port bound, blocking subsequent attempts to re-authenticate on the preferred port.

### Proposed Fix
Wrap the consent request in a try-finally block to ensure the callback server is closed:
```typescript
const callbackServer = startCallbackServer(pkceParams.state, preferredPort);
try {
  const userConsent = await getConsentForOauth(...);
  if (!userConsent) {
    throw new FatalCancellationError('Authentication cancelled by user.');
  }
} finally {
  if (!authenticated) {
    callbackServer.close(); // Ensure a cleanup method is exposed
  }
}
```

### Description
The core path resolution utility `resolveToRealPath` unconditionally decodes URI components using `decodeURIComponent` even if the path does not begin with the `file://` protocol:

```typescript
export function resolveToRealPath(pathStr: string): string {
  assertValidPathString(pathStr);
  let resolvedPath = pathStr;

  try {
    if (resolvedPath.startsWith('file://')) {
      resolvedPath = fileURLToPath(resolvedPath);
    }

    resolvedPath = decodeURIComponent(resolvedPath);
  } catch {
    // Ignore error (e.g. malformed URI), keep path from previous step
  }

  return robustRealpath(path.resolve(resolvedPath));
}
```

**Location**: [packages/core/src/utils/paths.ts#L422](https://github.com/google-gemini/gemini-cli/blob/main/packages/core/src/utils/paths.ts#L422)

### Why it's important
If a user has a file or directory with a literal `%` character followed by a hex code (such as `report%202026.txt` or a directory named `100%_complete`), `resolveToRealPath` will incorrectly decode `%20` to a space, searching for `report 2026.txt` which does not exist, causing a silent mismatch or `ENOENT` error. Conversely, if the path contains a literal `%` that is not a valid hex pair (e.g. `%xp`), it will throw a `URIError`, catch it, and keep the un-decoded version. 

### Proposed Fix
The utility should only perform `decodeURIComponent` inside the `if (resolvedPath.startsWith('file://'))` block:
```typescript
if (resolvedPath.startsWith('file://')) {
  resolvedPath = fileURLToPath(resolvedPath);
  resolvedPath = decodeURIComponent(resolvedPath);
}
```

